kubernetes-ocp uses WWW::Hetzner (Cloud API) for its Hetzner provider and has no skill for
using it. Working name: `perl-www-hetzner`.

## What the consumer calls today (kubernetes-ocp at 3b4c8c7)

- `WWW::Hetzner::Cloud->new(token => …)` — lib/OCP/Provider/Hetzner.pm, lib/OCP/Hetzner/Picker.pm, lib/OCP/Cmd/Hetzner/List.pm
- `->servers->create / get / delete / list / list_by_label / wait_for_status`
- `->ssh_keys->ensure($name, $pubkey)`
- `->locations->list`, `->server_types->list` (pickers)
- The returned `WWW::Hetzner::Cloud::Server` objects

Worth covering for consumers beyond that: the Cloud vs Robot split, the pluggable IO
transport and the mock fixtures (a consumer's network-free tests need them), what dies vs
what returns undef, and the per-location server type data (k33). Generic Hetzner platform
knowledge (networks, firewalls, IPs, costs) is `hetzner-operator@getty` — reference it,
don't repeat it.

## Why

This repo's own skill is a maintainer skill: it is written as "load before editing <this
dist>". A consumer project (here: kubernetes-ocp) needs the other half — how to *use* the
module correctly from outside — and must not install the maintainer skill, which would
fire on the wrong tasks. Precedent: kubernetes-rest ships `perl-kubernetes-rest` next to
`kubernetes-rest-core`; io-k8s-p5 ships `perl-io-k8s-kubernetes-classes` next to
`io-k8s-core`. The skill lives here because this repo owns its subject (skill
`getty-skill-library`, "Where a skill lives"); consumers install it as `<name>@<this-repo>`.

## Done when

- A tracked `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` exists here, its description written as a
  trigger for consumer work ("Use when calling <Module> from another project — …"), with
  no overlap with the maintainer skill (link to it for internals instead).
- The name follows the house naming rule (skill `getty-skill-library`, "Naming") and does
  not collide with anything in `skilletor available`. Working name below; the repo's
  agent decides.
- It covers the surface a consumer actually calls (list below), the argument and return
  shapes, how errors surface, and the traps a caller can step into — mined from the code
  and tests (skill `skill-mining`), not from memory.
- Wiring it into kubernetes-ocp is that board's card (see escalated-from), not this one.

